# MRL family 6: lazy quantifiers.
#
# A lazy quantifier's MINIMUM contribution to the bound is the same number
# as its greedy twin's (a{2,4}? and a{2,4} both need at least 2 bytes) --
# only the *search order* differs, not the bound. This family checks that
# MRL's bound computation is insensitive to greedy vs lazy, including when
# greedy and lazy are mixed across a nesting level (outer lazy/inner
# greedy and outer greedy/inner lazy are both covered, since they explore
# the same subject very differently).


# lazy quantifier immediately followed by a mandatory literal: since only one k in [2,4] can ever be followed by 'b', greedy and lazy converge to the same spans as family 1's a{2,4}b -- but the bound must still be computed correctly along pcrec's LAZY code path.
pattern a{2,4}?b
n "ab"
m "aab" 0 3
m "aaab" 0 4
m "aaaab" 0 5
m "aaaaab" 1 6

# LAZY inner nested in GREEDY outer: inner always prefers 1 byte per rep, so outer (wanting more reps, up to its max 5) absorbs any slack as MORE repetitions rather than longer ones. Overall min is 3 (outer min * inner min = 3*1).
pattern (a{1,3}?){3,5}
n "aa"
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 2 3
m "aaaa" 0 4
g 1 3 4
m "aaaaa" 0 5
g 1 4 5
m "aaaaaa" 0 5
g 1 4 5
m "aaaaaaaa" 0 5
g 1 4 5

# GREEDY inner nested in LAZY outer: outer prefers FEWER reps (tries its min, 3, first), and inner greedily front-loads whatever it can into those 3 reps before considering more reps at all.
pattern (a{1,3}){3,5}?
n "aa"
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 2 3
m "aaaaaa" 0 6
g 1 5 6
m "aaaaaaa" 0 7
g 1 6 7
m "aaaaaaaa" 0 8
g 1 6 8
